\section{Experimental Results}

\subsection{Convergence Analysis}

Table \ref{tab:convergence} shows global loss over 10 FL rounds.

\begin{table}[h]
\caption{Federated Learning Convergence (5 Classrooms)}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{cccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Round} & \textbf{Global Loss} & \textbf{Cumulative $\epsilon$} & \textbf{Comm. (MB)} \\
\midrule
0 (Initial) & 2.0349 & 0 & 0 \\
1 & 1.7841 & 9.60 & 44 \\
2 & 1.5382 & 19.19 & 88 \\
5 & 1.0251 & 47.99 & 220 \\
10 & 0.6714 & 95.97 & 440 \\
\bottomrule
\textbf{Reduction} & \textbf{67.0\%} & — & — \\
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
\label{tab:convergence}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{paper13_convergence.png}
\caption{FL Convergence: Privacy-Utility Tradeoff. The graph compares global loss reduction over 50 communication rounds for Baseline (Red), FedAvg without DP (Green), and FedAvg with DP ($\sigma=0.5$) (Blue). The DP model successfully tracks the non-private convergence curve with minimal lag.}
\label{fig:convergence}
\end{figure}

Loss decreases from 2.03 \textrightarrow 0.67 (67\% reduction), demonstrating successful convergence despite DP noise, as illustrated in Fig. \ref{fig:convergence}.

\subsection{Drift Compensation Efficacy}

Table \ref{tab:drift} compares accuracy with/without FL.

\begin{table}[h]
\caption{Model Drift with and without Federated Learning}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Scenario} & \textbf{No FL (Static)} & \textbf{With FL} & \textbf{Improvement} \\
\midrule
Lighting Drift & 80.2\% & 93.1\% & +12.9pp \\
Demographic Drift & 83.5\% & 92.8\% & +9.3pp \\
Seating Drift & 87.1\% & 94.2\% & +7.1pp \\
\midrule
\textbf{Average} & \textbf{85.7\%} & \textbf{93.2\%} & \textbf{+7.5pp} \\
\bottomrule
\multicolumn{4}{l}{\scriptsize Baseline accuracy (no drift): 95.0\%; FL reduces drift from 9.8\% to 2.0\%.}
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
\label{tab:drift}
\end{table}

FL reduces average drift from 9.8\% (95.0\% \textrightarrow 85.7\%) to 2.0\% (95.0\% \textrightarrow 93.2\%), a \textbf{79.6\% improvement}. Figure \ref{fig:drift_comparison} details this improvement across different classrooms.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{paper13_drift_comparison.png}
\caption{Model Drift Compensation: 79.6\% Improvement. Bar chart showing accuracy drop after 4 weeks for static models (Orange) vs. FedAvg+DP (Green) across 5 classrooms. Federated Learning consistently reduces accuracy loss to below 2.5\%.}
\label{fig:drift_comparison}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Privacy Sensitivity Analysis}

We performed a sensitivity analysis on the noise multiplier $\sigma$ to balance privacy and utility (Table \ref{tab:sensitivity}).

\begin{table}[h]
\caption{Privacy vs. Utility Trade-off Analysis}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{cccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Noise ($\sigma$)} & \textbf{Privacy ($\epsilon$)} & \textbf{Accuracy (Final)} & \textbf{Status} \\
\midrule
0.1 & $\epsilon > 1000$ & 94.8\% & Unsafe \\
\textbf{0.5} & \textbf{95.97} & \textbf{93.2\%} & \textbf{Selected} \\
1.0 & 42.15 & 88.4\% & Degraded \\
2.0 & 18.50 & 76.1\% & Unusable \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
\label{tab:sensitivity}
\end{table}



As shown, $\sigma=0.5$ provides the optimal balance, maintaining $\epsilon < 100$ while keeping accuracy above 93\%.

\subsection{Communication Cost}

Total communication over 10 rounds: 440 MB (gradients only). Compare to baseline retraining:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Baseline:} 12 TB/month (1080p video upload)
    \item \textbf{FedAvg:} 440 MB/month (gradients)
    \item \textbf{Reduction:} achieving ~27,000× bandwidth savings (12 TB / 440 MB ≈ 27,272×)
\end{itemize}

This makes FL economically viable even on constrained campus Wi-Fi \cite{b30}.

\subsection{Training Overhead}

Local training time: 8 minutes/round (5 epochs, ResNet-18 on Jetson Nano). Scheduled during non-class hours (overnight), zero impact on daytime inference \cite{b31}.

% ==========================================
% VIII. DISCUSSION
% ==========================================
\section{Discussion}

\subsection{Privacy-Utility Tradeoff}

Higher noise ($\sigma \uparrow$) strengthens privacy ($\epsilon \downarrow$) but slows convergence. We empirically selected $\sigma=0.5$ as the sweet spot: adequate convergence (10 rounds) while maintaining $\epsilon < 100$.

For stricter privacy (e.g., $\epsilon < 10$), options include:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Increase noise ($\sigma=5.0$): Requires 100+ rounds (impractical).
    \item Reduce sampling ($q=0.2$): Slower convergence, but $\epsilon$ scales linearly with $q$.
    \item Differential privacy amplification via shuffling \cite{b32}.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Comparison to Centralized Retraining}

Table \ref{tab:comparison} compares FL vs centralized approaches.

\begin{table}[h]
\caption{Federated Learning vs Centralized Retraining}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
\textbf{Metric} & \textbf{Centralized} & \textbf{FL (Ours)} \\
\midrule
Raw Data Transmission & 12 TB/month & 0 \\
Bandwidth Cost & \$2,400/month & \$0 \\
Privacy Bound & None & ($\epsilon=95.97, \delta=10^{-5}$) \\
GDPR Alignment & Risky & Operational Alignment \\
Convergence Rounds & 1 (faster) & 10 (slower) \\
Label Efficiency & 100\% reused & Active learning (15\%) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
\label{tab:comparison}
\end{table}

FL sacrifices convergence speed (10× slower) for privacy + cost savings (27,000× bandwidth reduction).

\subsection{Generalizability}

Our techniques generalize to other biometric edge deployments:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Retail:} Customer demographics tracking (GDPR-sensitive).
    \item \textbf{Healthcare:} Patient gait analysis (HIPAA-protected).
    \item \textbf{Smart Cities:} Crowd density estimation (privacy concerns).
\end{itemize}

Any deployment with: (1) distributed data, (2) privacy constraints, (3) model drift.

\subsection{Integration with Papers 11-12}

\textbf{Paper 11 Integration:} FL gradients leverage existing MQTT buffering (network resilience), mTLS security (gradient encryption), and OTA updates (model deployment).

\textbf{Paper 12 Integration:} FL checkpointing applies flash-aware optimizations (ZRAM, F2FS), preventing SD card wear during training.

This demonstrates \textit{system-level coherence} across all three papers.

% ==========================================
% IX. THREATS TO VALIDITY
% ==========================================
\section{Threats to Validity}

\textbf{Internal Validity.} Our simulation assumes teacher compliance with the verification UI. In deployment, label quality may vary (instructor fatigue, rushed responses). Mitigation: Multi-teacher consensus voting.

\textbf{External Validity.} Drift scenarios are synthetic (brightness reduction, occlusion masks). Real-world drift may be more complex (camera hardware failure, lighting flicker). Mitigation: Ongoing production deployment monitoring (50-classroom pilot).

\textbf{Construct Validity.} Privacy bounds rely on honest-but-curious server assumption. Malicious servers could attempt gradient inversion attacks \cite{b18}. Mitigation: Secure aggregation \cite{b16} (future work).

% ==========================================
% X. CONCLUSION
% ==========================================
\section{Conclusion}

This paper demonstrates that federated learning with differential privacy can compensate for model drift in edge biometric systems without violating data minimization principles. Our FedAvg+DP implementation reduces drift impact from 9.8\% to 2.0\% (79.6\% improvement) while maintaining ($\epsilon=95.97, \delta=10^{-5}$)-DP structural bounds—providing formal privacy accounting for compliance alignment rather than a claim of strong statistical indistinguishability—and achieving ~27,000× bandwidth savings versus centralized retraining (\$2,400/month \textrightarrow \$0).

Integration with our prior work \cite{b1, b9} provides a complete blueprint for production-grade Edge AI: deployment infrastructure, flash endurance, and privacy-preserving adaptation. The ScholarMaster system demonstrates an end-to-end educational edge AI architecture designed to operationally align with GDPR/DPDP data-minimization principles under the stated threat model while enabling multi-year deployments on commodity hardware. Federated adaptation operates strictly beyond the irreversible sensing boundary.

\textbf{Future Work.} We plan to: (1) Deploy in our 50-classroom pilot and validate against real drift data; (2) Implement secure aggregation for malicious-server defense; (3) Explore personalized federated learning (per-classroom model customization); (4) Extend to multi-modal drift (audio + video fusion).

